@page "/toolbar"

<CatHead Title="Toolbar">
    The filter bar that sits above a table or list. <code>.toolbar</code> is
    <code>space-between</code> and wraps; <code>.toolbar-filters</code> groups the controls on
    the leading side and <code>.toolbar-actions</code> the trailing one. Give it those two
    children and nothing else — a third floats in the middle.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Toolbar.FiltersCount" Title="Filters and a count" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.toolbar-input</code> narrows a <code>.form-input</code> to a sensible 160px so it
        does not eat the row. <code>.toolbar-count</code> is the muted result count.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Toolbar.SearchWidth" Title="A wider search box" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        160px is right for a filter dropdown and short for a search box: a search that matches
        across ten fields cannot fit its own placeholder, and the reader cannot see what they
        typed. <code>.toolbar-input--wide</code> is 280px, which is the case worth having a class
        for.
    </p>
    <p>
        The width is a knob, <code>--toolbar-input-width</code>, so a control between the two sizes
        sets it on the element — as the department select does here — rather than overriding
        <code>width</code> on a library class. <code>.toolbar-input--grow</code> takes whatever
        room is left instead, for a search box that is the only thing in the bar.
    </p>
    <p>
        A filter that carries a leading icon is an
        <a href="/form"><code>.input-group</code></a>, and
        <code>.toolbar-input</code> goes on the <em>group</em>: it is the group that has the width,
        because the <code>.form-input</code> inside it is <code>flex: 1</code>. A bare
        <code>&lt;i&gt;</code> beside the input is not the way — it is a flex item of its own and
        wraps away from the box it belongs to.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Toolbar.FiltersActions" Title="Filters and actions" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.toolbar-filters</code> and <code>.toolbar-actions</code> are the two groups, and
        <strong>a toolbar should have exactly these two children</strong>. <code>.toolbar</code> is
        <code>space-between</code>, so two children put one group at each end; a third floats in
        the middle, and <code>.sedna-push</code> does not rescue it because an auto margin and
        <code>space-between</code> are two answers to the same question.
    </p>
    <p>Put the buttons on the right; the primary action goes last.</p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Toolbar.HeadingAbove" Title="With a heading above it" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>The full top of a list page.</p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Toolbar.BulkSelection" Title="With a selection" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live — open the overflow menu.</strong> The same bar in its selected state: the count
        and the two ways out of it on the leading side, the actions that apply to the selection on the
        trailing side. Say how many are selected in text, and offer &ldquo;select all 128&rdquo;
        separately — selecting a page is not selecting a result set.
    </p>
    <p>
        The overflow is a <a href="/menu"><code>.menu-anchor</code></a> with
        <code>data-menu-toggle</code>. Anything destructive goes in there behind
        <code>.menu-item--danger</code>, and names the count it will act on.
    </p>
</CatExample>
